  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The central idea is to address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by systematically improving upon the initial solution through multi-resolution optimization and curve reconstruction. The bottleneck in the current approach is its heavy reliance on local search methods, which often get trapped in suboptimal regions. To overcome this, we introduce a hierarchical framework that:\n   - Uses grid refinement to better capture correlation behavior at different scales\n   - Employs analytic curve reconstruction from binary templates to generate smooth candidates\n   - Implements simulated tempering to break out of local minima across resolutions\n   - Combines gradient-based and combinatorial optimization phases\n\n   The selection rationale includes:\n   - The overlap objective is non-convex and highly multimodal\n   - The function shows better performance at grid points near multiples of \u03c0\n   - Multi-scale analysis can identify correlation features while minimizing computational cost\n   - Smooth templates tend to reduce discontinuous correlation spikes\n\n   Limitations include:\n   - Numerical stability constraints require explicit maximum bounds\n   - Phase transitions in the search space remain challenging\n   - Statistically significant improvement requires hundreds of function evaluations\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\n   Let h(x): [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] be a step function with weighted integral 1. We minimize:\n     C\u2085 = max_{k \u2208 Z} \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1\u2212h(x+k)) dx\n\n   Constraints:\n     \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\n   We use a three-stage framework:\n     A. Template generation: Exponential basis functions \u2113_j(x) = exp(\u2212x\u00b2/(\u03c3_j\u00b2)) shifted to [0,2]\n     B. Binary representation: h(x) = F(\u2211\u2099 \u03b2\u2099 \u2113\u2099(x)) clipped to [0,1]\n     C. Optimization: Minimize max_k C_k subject to \u222bh=1\n\n   The objective is evaluated via:\n     C\u2085 = max_k (1/2N) * (np.correlate(h, 1-h, 'full')[k+1] * 2.0/N)\n\n   Variables:\n     N: Cascade level (4 levels)\n     \u03b2: 3-dimensional grid of thresholds\n\n   Feasibility is ensured by:\n     FixSum: Gradual adjustment to satisfy \u222bh=1\n     GradProj: Project to [0,1] via sorted quantiles\n\n   Optimization uses:\n     LBFGS-B for continuous relaxation\n     Simulated tempering to escape local minima\n     Non-local means patch guidance for initialization\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\n   Stage 1: Template construction (150s)\n   - Seed with initial_h_values (380 points)\n   - Run multi-resolution bootstrap:\n        For n_points \u2208 [200, 500] in steps of 100:\n            Interpolate parent to new grid\n            Project for \u222bh=1\n            Guided binary search (800 evaluations)\n\n   Stage 2: Curve reconstruction (900s)\n   - Joint optimization of 4 cascade levels\n   - Exponential basis set \u2113_j(x) = exp(\u2212(x\u2212j\u00b70.1)\u00b2/(0.05j)\u00b2) for j=1,2,3\n   - Binary mapping via \u03b2 = \u0302z \u2212 0.5 (quantile-adjusted)\n   - Objective: min max_k (1/2N) * correlate\n\n   Stage 3: Final polish (50s)\n   - Multi-start with perturbed templates\n   - Coarse-to-fine optimization\n   - Final binary cleanup with integer programming\n\n4. Search choices:\n   Phase 1: Analytic basis: \n     \u2022 3-axis exponential templates\n     \u2022 \u03c3_j \u221d 0.05/j scaling for decay\n   Phase 2: Optimization settings:\n     \u2022 LBFGS-B with perturbed initial guesses\n     \u2022 Temperature schedule: T\u2080=1.5, drop=0.95\n     \u2022 Move set: \n         1. Stochastic basis shifts\n         2. Binary patch flipping\n         3. Local projection adjustments\n   Phase 3: Judging mechanism:\n     \u2022 Update rule: candidate accepted if C\u2085 improves significantly\n     \u2022 Validation: 10 delayed restarts per candidate\n     \u2022 Early termination if no improvement in 500 evaluations\n\n5. Compute budget:\n   - Total CPU time: 800s (150s setup, 500s optimization, 150s polish)\n   - Deadline check within top-level descent iterations\n   - Each LBFGS iteration: parent template evaluation\n   - Each binary move: partial correlation update (~100 correlation evals/sec)\n\n   Estimated memory: 1.2GB for storing gradient estimates\n   Hierarchical fallback: Residual analysis at coarser level\n\n6. Validation and contract:\n   - Phase final: Cross-validation of objective\n   - Sliding window monitor of C\u2085k values\n   - Final output checks:\n        \u222bh=1 to tolerance 1e-5\n        C\u2085 computed via np.correlate\n        Input seed preserved\n   - Output statistics:\n        h_array: float32 of length n_points\n        c5_bound: float (objective value)\n        n_points: integer (current resolution)",
